/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Recap in three. Continuous time has a value at every instant; discrete time, only at whole numbers. A pulse has finite energy, an endless sine finite power. And even plus odd gives the signal back, energies too.',cues:{r1:'Continuous time has',r2:'A pulse has',r3:'And even plus odd'},panels:[["Signals and systems","BLU",'sgCtdt',['x(t): a value at every real t.','x[n]: values at integer n only.'],'r1'],["Size and repeats","ORG",'sgSize',['Pulse: finite E. Endless sine: finite P.','Periodic: repeats every T₀.'],'r2'],["Even and odd","GRN",'sgEvenOdd',['x = xₑ + xₒ, with xₑ = (x(t) + x(−t))/2.','1, 2, 3: E = 14 = 7.5 + 6.5.'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is a discrete-time signal x[n] not defined at n = 2.5?",say:'Question one. Why is a discrete-time signal x of n not defined at n equals two point five?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why is a'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is a discrete-time signal x[n] not defined at n = 2.5?",say:'Because n is an index, not a time. The sequence holds one value per whole number. Two point five falls between the samples at two and three, and nothing is there.',cues:{a1:'Because n is',a2:'The sequence holds',a3:'Two point five falls'},al:[["n is an index, not a time.",'a1'],["The sequence holds one value per integer n only.",'a2'],["n = 2.5 falls between the samples at n = 2 and n = 3, and a DT signal has no point there.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),SG_L(40,300,580,300,'#ADB5BD',2)+SG_stems(SG_dt4.slice(0,6),70,100,300,45,ORG,8)+[0,1,2,3,4,5].map(n=>SG_T(70+n*100,336,String(n),{fs:24,a:'middle',fill:'#5C6577'})).join('')+SG_T(580,380,'n (sample index)',{fs:22,a:'end',fill:'#5C6577'})+SG_T(176,124,'3.80 V',{fs:22,fill:'#5C6577'}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),SG_L(320,300,320,140,TRED,4,'stroke-dasharray="8 8"')+SG_T(320,126,'?',{fs:44,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED})+SG_T(320,430,'n = 2.5: no sample',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED})+SG_X(320,300,16,TRED))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A sequence is x[n] = 3 cos(0.25πn) V. Find x[0], x[2] and x[4], with units.",say:'Question two. Take three cosine of zero point two five pi n volts. Find x of zero, x of two and x of four, with units.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'Take three cosine'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A sequence is x[n] = 3 cos(0.25πn) V. Find x[0], x[2] and x[4], with units.",say:'At n equals zero, cosine of zero is one: three volts. At n equals two the angle is ninety degrees, cosine zero: zero volts. At n equals four the angle is pi, cosine minus one: minus three volts.',cues:{fk:'At n equals zero',k1:'cosine of zero is one',k2:'At n equals two',k2b:'cosine zero',k3:'At n equals four',k3b:'minus three volts'},calc:{f:['x[n] = 3 cos(0.25πn) V'],hint:'Angle = 0.25π × n radians.',fk:'fk',steps:[["n = 0","x[0] = 3 cos 0 = 3 V","k1"],["n = 2",["0.25π × 2 = 0.5π rad = 90°","x[2] = 3 × 0 = 0 V"],["k2","k2b"]],["n = 4",["0.25π × 4 = π rad = 180°","x[4] = 3 × (−1) = −3 V"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A student claims every DT signal comes from sampling a CT signal. Test the claim and state what it implies for the order of study.",say:'Question three. A student claims every discrete signal comes from sampling a continuous one. Test the claim, and say what it means for the order of study.',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'A student claims'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A student claims every DT signal comes from sampling a CT signal. Test the claim and state what it implies for the order of study.",say:'The claim is false. Daily counts and closing prices are discrete by nature, and three cosine of zero point two five pi n has no continuous parent. Sampling is one route, not the definition. So discrete signals stand on their own; the sampling theorem links the two.',cues:{a1:'The claim is false',a2:'Sampling is one route',a3:'So discrete signals'},al:[["False: daily counts and prices are discrete by nature, and x[n] = 3 cos(0.25πn) has no CT parent.",'a1'],["Sampling is one route to a DT signal, not its definition.",'a2'],["So DT signals are studied in their own right; the sampling theorem links the two types.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),SG_box(10,14,600,140,{fill:'#FFFFFF'})+SG_curve(t=>Math.sin(2*Math.PI*t),0,1.5,30,190,84,40,BLU,4)+SG_arrow(200,84,250,84)+SG_box(256,56,120,56,{fill:'#E3FAF1',st:ACC,sw:3,r:10})+SG_T(316,92,'sampler',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle'})+SG_arrow(382,84,430,84)+SG_stems([0,1,2,3,4,5].map(k=>Math.sin(2*Math.PI*k*0.25)*0.9),450,28,84,40,ORG,6)+SG_T(30,146,'route 1: sample a CT signal',{fs:22,fill:'#5C6577'}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),SG_box(10,174,600,140,{fill:'#FFFFFF'})+[0,1,2,3].map(i=>[0,1].map(j=>`<rect x="${34+i*40}" y="${196+j*40}" width="32" height="32" rx="5" fill="#DBE4FF" stroke="${BLU}" stroke-width="2"/>`).join('')).join('')+SG_arrow(200,240,250,240)+SG_T(260,232,'daily count',{fs:22,fw:700})+SG_arrow(382,240,430,240)+SG_stems([0.5,0.9,0.4,0.8,0.6,1].map(v=>v*0.9),450,28,268,50,ORG,6)+SG_T(30,306,'discrete by nature',{fs:22,fill:'#5C6577'}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1')+1.2,SG_box(10,334,600,140,{fill:'#FFFFFF'})+SG_T(30,412,'3 cos(0.25πn)',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU})+SG_arrow(232,404,430,404)+SG_stems([0,1,2,3,4,5].map(n=>Math.cos(0.25*Math.PI*n)*0.9),450,28,404,40,ORG,6)+SG_T(30,466,'a formula, no CT parent',{fs:22,fill:'#5C6577'}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes for this lesson are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the sampled sine and the even and odd split from memory, then check them.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the sampled'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The two chapters", "Signals and systems defined; CT and DT signals; Classification: energy, power, periodic, even and odd signals"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The sampled sine", "x(t) = 4 sin(20πt) V has a value at every instant; sampling it every 10 ms keeps only x[n] = x(0.01n). Marked: x(0.025 s) = 4 V and x[5] = 0 V."], ["The even and odd split", "x[n] = 1, 2, 3 split into its even part and its odd part; the parts add back to x[n], and their energies 7.5 and 6.5 add to 14."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: Alan V. Oppenheim (Signals and Systems); Simon Haykin (Signals and Systems); B. P. Lathi (Linear Systems and Signals)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: continuous time has a value at every instant, discrete time lives at whole numbers, and every signal splits into even and odd. Next time, standard signals and operations on signals. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'continuous time has',w2:'discrete time lives',w3:'every signal splits',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['A VALUE AT EVERY t','BLU','w1'],['SAMPLES AT INTEGER n','ORG','w2'],['EVEN + ODD = THE SIGNAL','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Standard signals · Operations on signals", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
